21;1H(B[0;1m[36m```bash[22d[33m# Basic run[23d[36mpython3 benchmark.py --runs 5[39m(B[m[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[22;1H(B[0;1m[33m# With report output[23d[36mpython3 benchmark.py --runs 5 --report[39m(B[m[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[22;1H(B[0;1m[33m# Compare normal vs CPU stressed[23d[36mpython3 benchmark.py --compare --runs 5 --report[39m(B[m[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[22;1H(B[0;1m[33m# Compare all models normal vs CPU stressed[23d[36mpython3 benchmark.py --compare-models --runs 3 --report[39m(B[m[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[22;1H(B[0;1m[33m# Compare all models under memory pressure[23d[36mpython3 benchmark.py --memory-stress --runs 3 --report[39m(B[m[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1H(B[0;1m[36m```[23d[33m---[39m(B[m[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[22;1H(B[0;1m[33m## Key Findings[23d[39m(B[m[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1H(B[0;1m[33m### CPU Stress Results[22d[35m![CPU Stress Report][34m(model_report.png)[23d[39m(B[m[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1H| Model[21;22H| Normal | Stressed | Slowdown  |[22d|--------------------|--------|----------|-----------|[23d| LogisticRegression | 5.5ms  | 2047ms   | +37,253%  |[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1H| RandomForest[22G| 123ms  | 136ms    | +10.5%    |[22d| GradientBoosting   | 199ms  | 224ms    | +12.7%    |[23d| SVM[23;22H| 41ms   | 36ms     | -12.2%    |[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[22;1H(B[0;1m[32m**Why:**[39m(B[m LogisticRegression uses an iterative solver — thousands of[23dsequential steps that each get delayed under CPU contention, and the[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1Hdelays stack up. RandomForest builds trees in parallel so one delayed[22dthread doesn't block the others.[23d[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1H(B[0;1m[33m---[23d### Memory Stress Results[39m(B[m[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1H(B[0;1m[35m![Memory Stress Report][34m(memory_stress_report.png)[23d[39m(B[m| Model[23;22H| Normal | Stressed | Slowdown  |[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1H|--------------------|--------|----------|-----------|[22d| RandomForest[22G| 149ms  | 1049ms   | +600%     |[23d| LogisticRegression | 3.2ms  | 610ms    | +19,226%  |[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1H| SVM[21;22H| 38ms   | 152ms    | +297%     |[22d| GradientBoosting   | 414ms  | 949ms    | +129%     |[23d[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1H(B[0;1m[32m**Why:**[39m(B[m RandomForest builds 50 trees simultaneously, each holding its[22down data in RAM. Under memory pressure it constantly swaps data in and[23dout — called thrashing. GradientBoosting builds trees one at a time so[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1Hit only needs one tree worth of RAM at any moment.[23d(B[0;1m[33m---[39m(B[m[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[22;1H(B[0;1m[33m### Conclusion[23d[39m(B[m[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1H[35m> The fastest model in ideal conditions can be the slowest in production.[22d> Always benchmark under realistic system conditions.[23d[39m(B[m[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1H| If your server is...     | Best model choice  |[22d|--------------------------|--------------------|[23d| CPU constrained[23;28H| GradientBoosting   |[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1H| Memory constrained[28G| GradientBoosting   |[22d| Ideal conditions[22;28H| LogisticRegression |[23d| Unknown / variable load  | GradientBoosting   |[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[22;1H(B[0;1m[33m---[23d[39m(B[m[?25l[?12l[?25h7[2;24r8[24d[3S[1;26r[21;1H(B[0;1m[33m## Requirements[23d[36m```bash[39m